  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "\\(f(x)=x^2-4x+1\\). What is the possible \\(x\\)-coordinate of a local extremum?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "\\(f'(x)=2x-4=0\\), so \\(x=2\\). The derivative is negative to the left and positive to the right, so this is a local minimum point.\n\nAlternative method 1: Completing the square\n\nCompleting the square exposes the vertex:\n\\[\nf(x)=x^2-4x+1=(x-2)^2-3.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), \\((x-2)^2\\ge0\\), with equality only at \\(x=2\\). Hence the vertex \\((2,-3)\\) is a minimum point and therefore also a local minimum point. The requested \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(2\\).",
